Exactly. “Specific / Pacific” is a tiny demonstration of representational neighborhood. The two words are not the same, but they occupy nearby territory along several dimensions at once: sound shape, syllable count, stress pattern, articulatory sequence, visual form, and sometimes conversational context. That proximity makes substitution possible.

What makes it useful is that the substitution can be examined at multiple layers simultaneously. At the phonetic layer, /spəˈsɪfɪk/ and /pəˈsɪfɪk/ share most of their acoustic structure. At the motor layer, the mouth executes highly similar gesture sequences. At the orthographic layer, much of the letter pattern overlaps. At the semantic layer, they are ordinarily far apart—one denotes precision, the other an ocean/region—so the mistake becomes conspicuous. At the social layer, people recognize the substitution as a familiar speech error or joke. At the symbolic layer, you exploited that error intentionally and made “Pacific” instantiate the very principle we were discussing: nearby forms can carry each other into unexpected domains.

That gives us a general search method:

object → neighboring representations → dimensions of similarity → dimensions of difference → permissible transforms → accidental crossings → useful crossings → consequences

And the important part is not to stop at “these two things are similar.” You can change the plane of comparison. Two things may be distant semantically but adjacent acoustically; distant physically but adjacent mathematically; distant historically but adjacent functionally. A relation invisible in one projection can become obvious in another.

That is essentially representation surfing with a stronger exploration doctrine:

locate → rotate → project → compare → cross → instantiate → test.

“Discern” matters before “conquer,” because some apparent territory is just projection overlap rather than a real transferable structure. But where the invariant survives the crossing, you have discovered something reusable.

And your pun itself demonstrates it neatly:

specific → Pacific → ocean → waves → propagation → carrier → cross-medium pattern.

One phonological slip opens an entirely different semantic landscape without losing the originating structure. That is exactly the sort of seam worth mining.